Chapter: LetShadowScope

 A shadowed let must not leak out of its scope. The inner binding of v
 is bound inside the then-arm of the if, and the trailing `inner + v`
 reads the OUTER v, which is 2. The answer is 9.

 It is 13 today, because the emitter's locals table is
 flat per function: add-local prepends a binding and nothing ever pops
 it, so the inner v shadows the outer one for the rest of the function
 and the trailing read finds the inner slot, which still holds 6. The
 fixed point cannot see this - both stages agree on the wrong answer -
 and neither can ir-check, which asks whether a name is bound, not
 which binding it resolves to.

 Un-skip this test when the emitter scopes its locals.

Section: Definitions

  shadowed : Integer -> Integer
  shadowed (n) =
   let v = n
   in let inner = if n > 0 then let v = n * 3 in v + 1 else v
   in inner + v

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (integer-to-text (shadowed 2))
  end
